  },
  "content": {
   "rendered": "<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media-detail-hero nasa-gb-align-wide width-full maxw-full margin-y-5 desktop:margin-y-7 padding-x-3 media-detail-hero--suppress-inner-media-caption hds-module alignwide wp-block-nasa-blocks-media-detail-hero\">\t<div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block padding-x-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"label margin-bottom-1\">APOD</p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"heading-22 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-2\">Astronomy Picture of the Day</p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3 media-detail-hero__subtext\">\n\t\t\t\t<a href=\"/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"media-detail-hero__media margin-bottom-6\">\n\t\t\t\t<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-full\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \"><a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance. (opens in a new tab)\"><img decoding=\"async\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 85rem) calc(100vw - 3rem), 85rem\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=1280&amp;h=1280&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1280w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=319&amp;h=319&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 319w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=300&amp;h=300&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=400&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 400w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=600&amp;h=600&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 600w\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1525\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Moonsaturn6_Ortu_2124.jpg?w=2048&amp;h=1525&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" class=\"attachment-2048x1525 size-2048x1525\" alt=\"The curving surface of the Moon occupies the lower half of the image. Just above center the planet Saturn appears, far in the distance.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<h1 class=\"display-48 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3\">Saturn Behind the Moon</h1>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-row media-detail-hero__content-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-0 media-detail-hero__description\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<strong>Explanation:</strong> Sometimes Saturn disappears. It doesn&#8217;t really go away, of course &#8212; it just <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-april-29-saturns-rings-appear-to-disappear/\">disappears</a> from our view when <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-september-5-earth-and-moon/\">Earth&#8217;s Moon</a> moves in front. Such a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/\">Saturn</a>ian eclipse, more formally called an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/\">occultation</a>, was visible in early 2025 along a small <a href=\"https://in-the-sky.org/news/occultations/occultation_20250104_1652_visibility.png\" rel=\"noopener\">swath</a> of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/\">Earth</a> &#8212; from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenland\" rel=\"noopener\">Greenland</a> through central <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe\" rel=\"noopener\">Europe</a> and into northern <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Africa\" rel=\"noopener\">Africa</a>. The <a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/194524290@N05/54963364007/\" rel=\"noopener\">featured image</a> is a digital fusion of three images, the first of just <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-23-cassini-approaches-saturn/\">Saturn</a> before it disappeared, and then two more of the Moon with <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2020-august-24-crescent-moon-hdr/\">different exposure</a> lengths. The images were taken from <a href=\"https://youtu.be/z6hf3TY5LZU\" rel=\"noopener\">Sardinia</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy\" rel=\"noopener\">Italy</a>, after sunset. The bright dot near Saturn is not a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-august-11-six-moons-of-saturn/\">moon of Saturn</a>, but rather the star <a href=\"https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/85_Aquarii\" rel=\"noopener\">85 Aquarii</a>, which is further behind Saturn than Saturn is behind the Moon. The next time the <a href=\"https://svs.gsfc.nasa.gov/5587/\" rel=\"noopener\">Moon</a> moves in front of Saturn, as visible from <a href=\"https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRI9kyfDjt8-b4RVVADhbiFfBl5MR8Rl16peGoUYAuP0PwITdzj7rYB1EnN\" rel=\"noopener\">some place on Earth</a>, will be in 2031. <br><br><strong>Tomorrow&#8217;s picture: </strong>small but mighty\t\t\t\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-none desktop:display-block desktop:grid-col-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5 margin-top-3 desktop:margin-top-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<table class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-table width-full\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Date</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tNovember 10, 2026\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Credit &amp; Copyright</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/194524290@N05/\" rel=\"noopener\">Roberto Ortu</a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Authors &amp; editors:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">A service of:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/astrophysics/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASD</a> at <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GSFC</a>,<br /><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/about-science-activation/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA Science Activation</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Michigan Tech. U.</a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</table>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t</div>\n\n"
