   "rendered": "APOD: 2026 October 28 &#8211; The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io"
  },
  "content": {
   "rendered": "<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media-detail-hero nasa-gb-align-wide width-full maxw-full margin-y-5 desktop:margin-y-7 padding-x-3 media-detail-hero--suppress-inner-media-caption hds-module alignwide wp-block-nasa-blocks-media-detail-hero\">\t<div class=\"grid-container grid-container-block padding-x-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"label margin-bottom-1\">APOD</p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"heading-22 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-2\">Astronomy Picture of the Day</p>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3 media-detail-hero__subtext\">\n\t\t\t\t<a href=\"/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"media-detail-hero__media margin-bottom-6\">\n\t\t\t\t<div id=\"\" class=\"width-full maxw-full margin-left-auto margin-right-auto hds-media-align-full hds-module wp-block-nasa-blocks-video\"><div class=\"hds-cover-wrapper width-full maxw-full flex-column\"><div class=\"hds-video-container width-full embed-container\" style=\"\"><video title=\"Ganymede Occultation of Io\" id=\"nasa-plus-GnFRq\" class=\"video-js video-player vjs-fluid width-full\" data-setup='{&quot;controls&quot;:true,&quot;preload&quot;:&quot;auto&quot;}' data-nasa-video-analytics='{&quot;debug&quot;:false,&quot;data&quot;:{&quot;env_key&quot;:&quot;91nns8oppqdfqc44lgo4b1gni&quot;,&quot;player_name&quot;:&quot;www.nasa.gov Player&quot;,&quot;video_name&quot;:&quot;Ganymede Occultation of Io&quot;}}'  poster=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.jpeg/jcr:content/renditions/cq5dam.web.1280.1280.jpeg\" ><source src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/occultation.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"><p class=\"vjs-no-js\">To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that\n\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://videojs.com/html5-video-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">supports HTML5 video</a></p></video></div></div><div class=\"hds-media-caption hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-credits\"><div>Michael Wong</div></div></div></div>\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<h1 class=\"display-48 margin-top-0 margin-bottom-3\">The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io</h1>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-row media-detail-hero__content-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<p class=\"p-md margin-0 media-detail-hero__description\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<strong>Explanation:</strong> Jupiter is one of the least lonely planets in the Solar System, at least when it comes to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/\">natural satellites</a>. <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/how-many-moons/en/\" rel=\"noopener\">115 moons</a> have been discovered orbiting Jupiter so far! (<em>Saturn has the most with 293, but <a href=\"https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/sats/discovery.html\" rel=\"noopener\">who’s counting</a>.</em>) <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/ganymede/\">Ganymede</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/jupiter/jupiter-moons/io/\">Io</a>, two of Jupiter’s <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/415-years-ago-astronomer-galileo-discovers-jupiters-moons/\" rel=\"noopener\">Galilean moons</a>, met in the sky on October 20th, 2026. Today’s video captures the meeting, with <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2021-june-14-ganymede-from-juno/\">Ganymede</a> briefly blocking <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-20-io-in-true-color/\">Io</a> from view. This event, when an astronomical object passes in front of another and either completely or partially blocks it, is called a mutual occultation. The <a href=\"https://www.alpo-astronomy.org/jupiter\" rel=\"noopener\">mutual occultations of Galilean moons</a> are rare to witness because they require Earth’s edge-on view of their orbits. This occurs once every 6 years when Jupiter reaches <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/jupiter-equinox-mutual-events-moon-2021/\" rel=\"noopener\">its equinox</a> and appears less “tilted”. Ganymede is also <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2022-april-27-moon-shadow-on-jupiter/\">casting its shadow</a> on Jupiter’s right side. Any <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jupiter_in_fiction\" rel=\"noopener\">fictional Jovian species</a> within the shadow would experience a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-17-a-golden-corona-eclipse/\">solar eclipse</a>!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow&#8217;s picture:</strong> a planet grows horns\t\t\t\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"display-none desktop:display-block desktop:grid-col-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div>\n\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-5 margin-top-3 desktop:margin-top-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<table class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-table width-full\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Date:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tOctober 28, 2026\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Credit &amp; Copyright:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://www.astromw.com/\" rel=\"noopener\">Michael Wong</a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">Authors &amp; editors:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"media-detail-hero__meta-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<th scope=\"row\" class=\"label padding-y-1\">A service of:</th>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<td class=\"p-sm padding-y-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/astrophysics/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASD</a> at <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NASA</a> / <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GSFC</a>,<br><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/learn/about-science-activation/\" target=\"_blank\">NASA Science Activation</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.mtu.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Michigan Tech. U.</a>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</tbody>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t</table>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t</div>\n\n"
  },
